HERE IS THE MODELS COMMENTS AFTER SEEING ALL ABOVE STATEMENTS. DO NOT USE THIS AS A DEISNG CHOICE DOCUMENT, OR ANY INDICATION THAT I WANT WHAT IS MENTIONED. USE THIS FOR CONTEXT ONLY. 

Yes. After reviewing the full collection in STATEMENT DESIGN ALL STATEMENTS, the biggest conclusion is that this should not be designed as a collection of bank-specific regex extractors. It needs to be a document-understanding pipeline with explicit statement assembly, layout classification, row reconstruction, validation, provenance, and privacy controls.
1. Treat statement assembly as a separate problem

Before extracting transactions, the tool needs to determine:

Which pages belong to the same statement
Which account or card section a page belongs to
Whether a page is a summary, transaction page, continuation page, payment advice, rewards page, terms page, correspondence page, or blank page
Whether page numbering restarts within the file
Whether unnumbered pages belong to the preceding statement
Whether statements are presented out of chronological order
Whether one file contains several products, accounts, cardholders, statement eras, or currencies

This is essential because the source set includes:

Files containing many statements
Separate terms or payment pages outside the printed page count
Payment-options pages between summary and transaction pages
Continuation pages that do not repeat headings
Files containing non-statement correspondence
Composite statements with multiple accounts
Spreadsheet workbooks with one account per sheet
Product or card-network changes within the same account history
Files whose physical page order is not reliably chronological

A transaction parser should never be responsible for discovering these boundaries while simultaneously parsing rows. Create a statement manifest first, then extract each assembled statement.

2. Separate four concepts that are currently easy to conflate

The tool should distinguish:

Institution
Layout family
Product label
Layout era or variant

For example, a product name can change while the transaction layout remains stable. Conversely, the same product can move from an older layout to a newer layout with different headings, page order, balance conventions and foreign-currency representation. Card networks can also change without creating a new account or necessarily requiring a new logical parser.

I would use identifiers such as:

Bank family
Layout-family ID
Variant ID
Product label as printed
Variant effective evidence
Confidence
Recognition evidence

The product name should be metadata, not the primary parser selector.

3. Use hierarchical recognition, not one flat list of phrases

Recognition should happen in stages:

Stage 1: source type

Identify:

Text PDF
Scanned or image PDF
Mixed PDF
XLS
XLSX or XLSM
CSV
Processed analytical dataset rather than original bank output
Stage 2: broad family

Use stable combinations such as:

Institution wording
Table-heading signature
Statement title
Summary labels
Known terminators
Stage 3: layout variant

Resolve differences such as:

Older versus newer headings
Capitalisation and punctuation changes
Mastercard versus Visa wording
Summary page versus transaction page ordering
One signed amount column versus separate debit and credit columns
Stage 4: statement instance

Identify:

Period
account-level metadata
restarted page number
product wording
section membership

A recogniser should retain the phrases and coordinates that caused a classification. That makes misclassification explainable and testable.

4. Do not rely on words alone

The strongest common pattern across the collection is that text content is not sufficient. The parser should use multiple signals:

Text
Page coordinates
Column positions
Font size and emphasis
Horizontal alignment
Repeated header locations
Page dimensions and orientation
Spreadsheet cell coordinates
Spreadsheet data types
Spreadsheet number formats
Merged-cell structure
Hidden-sheet state
Formula values and formula text
OCR bounding boxes and confidence

This matters because several designs have unlabelled visual columns, visually spanning headings, icons instead of category text, description text split across adjacent cells, and text extraction orders that do not follow the printed reading order.

For spreadsheets specifically, never flatten nonblank cells into sequential text. Blank cells preserve column meaning.

5. Model transaction rows as structured objects with source fragments

A transaction should not initially be represented as a single line of text. It should be assembled from fragments.

Suggested core fields:

Statement ID
Account-section ID
Source page or sheet
Source row or bounding region
Posted date
Processed or value date
Time
Description
Reference or particulars
Counterparty
Transaction type or code
Debit
Credit
Signed amount
Running balance
Currency
Foreign amount
Foreign exchange rate
Card suffix
Section or cardholder
Row status
Extraction confidence
Validation flags
Raw source fragments

Keep the raw fragments and their coordinates. A later normalisation step can produce a clean description without destroying the evidence needed to debug row assembly.

6. Make continuation-line handling a first-class feature

Multi-line rows are normal, not exceptional. Continuation content can include:

Additional description
Particulars and references
Counterparty information
Masked card details
Original transaction date
Foreign amount and currency
Exchange rate
Conversion fee
Processed date
Payment allocation
Loan-component information
Location and terminal detail

A robust row assembler should work from anchors:

A new valid transaction date
A populated transaction amount in a recognised money column
A known table boundary
A known continuation phrase
A new section heading
A significant coordinate reset

Do not assume the amount is always on the first line or always on the last line. Both patterns appear in the collection.

7. Direction must come from structure before semantics

This is probably the single most important transaction-classification rule.

Priority should generally be:

Populated debit or credit column
Signed amount representation
Explicit DR, CR, OD or bracket convention
Type column with a documented legend
Description semantics only as a validation signal

Descriptions are not dependable enough to determine direction. Refunds can carry purchase-like codes, a bill-payment code can appear in either money column, adjustments can be either debit or credit, and loan exports can invert the intuitive meaning of increases and repayments.

Never override a clear printed column position because the description “sounds” like a credit or debit.

8. Use a proper money parser with contextual rules

The corpus contains many monetary shapes:

Separate debit and credit columns
One amount column with trailing CR
One signed numeric amount column
Leading minus before the currency symbol
Trailing minus
Trailing DR
Trailing OD
Brackets
No currency symbol
Leading currency symbol
Currency symbol in a heading rather than each value
Credit balance with CR
Zero without either sign
Spreadsheet number formats that display OD or brackets without changing the stored value
A literal period used instead of a balance

The parsed money object should retain:

Raw printed value
Numeric value, when valid
Currency symbol
Currency code, if available
Sign convention
Debit or credit interpretation
Whether the sign came from the raw value, column, type field or number format
Whether the value was missing, blank, zero or nonnumeric

Do not convert ".", "-" or blank to zero unless the specific layout says that is correct.

9. Running-balance validation should be configurable, not absolute

Balance checking is extremely valuable, but it cannot be a universal gate.

Possible modes:

Strict
Tolerant
Informational
Disabled

Reasons balance validation can fail legitimately include:

Statement has no running balance
Balance appears only on some rows
Balance is formula-derived
Spreadsheet formulas are unavailable
Card authorisation and billing timing differ
OCR has reordered rows
A continuation row has been mistaken for a transaction
Rejected or NSF rows show an amount without changing the balance
Zero-value account events repeat the balance
Loan balances use different economic semantics
Data exports warn that their balance may not match apparent transaction chronology
Some rows are missing from the supplied file

A balance mismatch should produce a diagnostic, not silently delete or mutate transactions.

10. Use multiple layers of reconciliation

Where available, validate at several levels:

Row level
Previous balance plus signed amount equals new balance
Debit and credit are not both populated unless explicitly supported
Date and money shapes are plausible
Page level
Page withdrawals and deposits match printed page totals
Carried-forward balance matches the next page’s brought-forward balance
Statement level
Opening balance plus credits minus debits equals closing balance
Parsed transaction totals match printed totals
Period-end balance matches the printed closing balance
Section level
Cardholder total matches transactions in the cardholder section
Account-section turnover matches account-section rows
Loan component totals match applicable summary fields
Workbook level
Each account sheet remains independent
Hidden or explanatory sheets produce no transactions
Calculated balance columns agree where intended

Each check should return pass, fail, unavailable or not applicable.

11. Zero-value rows must be preserved

The collection repeatedly shows legitimate zero-value rows:

Account opening or closure events
Enquiries
Rejected transactions
Rate changes
Repayment changes
Offset attachment or detachment
Status events
Foreign-currency detail rows
Interest adjustment events
Calculation-support artefacts that must be excluded for structural reasons, not merely because they are zero

“Amount equals zero” is therefore not a safe transaction-removal rule.

Instead, classify rows as:

Monetary transaction
Zero-value account event
Continuation detail
Page-control row
Statement-control row
Section heading
Summary row
Explanatory text
Workbook instruction
Unknown row
12. Avoid aggressive deduplication

Several valid patterns look duplicated:

Debit and matching credit with identical descriptions
Reversal next to original transaction
Repayment and re-borrow for the same amount
Multiple fees with the same date and amount
Internal transfer printed in two account sections
Multiple loan components with matching dates
Repeated account-fee rows
A payment duplicated across cardholder and account-level contexts only in appearance

Deduplication should require strong evidence such as identical source identity, page overlap caused by file duplication, or a documented duplicate marker. Matching date, amount and description is not enough.

13. Statement dates require a dedicated year-resolution engine

Many transaction rows omit the year. The year must often be inferred from:

Statement opening date
Statement closing date
Month rollover
Processed date
Row order
Adjacent rows
Whether a transaction date may precede the period start while processing occurred inside the period

The engine should support cross-year statements and should not reject a transaction merely because its transaction date falls just outside the stated period. That occurs legitimately in credit-card statements.

Store both:

Printed date
Resolved date
Resolution method
Resolution confidence
14. OCR correction must be conservative and field-aware

The source set contains OCR risks such as:

O versus 0
l versus 1
S versus 5
Missing spaces in dates
Spaces inside decimal values
Missing year digits
Merged words
Reordered rows
Split numbers
Stray technical artefacts
Image-only pages within otherwise text-based files

Do not globally replace characters. Apply corrections only within a field already classified as a date, amount, balance or page number.

For example, converting l0Apr to 10 Apr may be reasonable in a date column, but not in a merchant description.

Every OCR correction should preserve the original token and record the transformation.

15. Build explicit page and section classifiers

The tool should have classifiers for at least:

Statement summary
Transaction page
Transaction continuation page
Payment advice or payment slip
Rewards summary
Interest-rate summary
Terms and conditions
Promotional content
Account portfolio summary
Loan summary
Fee summary
Correspondence
Footnotes or code legend
Blank or unusable page
Unknown

Similarly, within a transaction page:

Cardholder section
General payments and charges
Sundry account transactions
Account section
Currency section
Loan section

This will prevent totals, rewards, credit limits, payment instructions, fee summaries and portfolio balances from becoming false transactions.

16. Treat payment slips and repeated summaries as duplicate evidence, not new data

Many designs repeat:

Statement period
Closing balance
Minimum payment
Due date
Account number
Card number
Customer details

These repetitions are useful for validation and statement assembly. They should not create additional balances or transactions.

The same principle applies to:

Carried forward
Brought forward
Previous balance
Payment advice
Closing balance repeated below a table
Summary values printed again in detachable sections

Record repeated values as supporting observations linked to one canonical statement field.

17. Distinguish transaction tables from secondary money tables

A page can contain several tables with amounts and dates:

Transactions
Interest-rate summary
Loan-fee summary
Outstanding payment
Rewards
Portfolio balances
Upcoming automatic payments
Payment advice
Foreign-currency account summary
Account totals
Loan repayment schedule

Recognition must be table-specific. Finding dates and money on a page does not mean finding transactions.

A useful safeguard is to require a recognised transaction-table state before creating transaction rows.

18. Preserve source semantics before standardising

The final normalised output can use common fields, but the original semantics should remain available.

Examples:

Posted date versus effective date
Transaction date versus processed date
Balance versus principal balance
Arrears balance versus account balance
Amount versus debit and credit
Payment method versus transaction type
Card used versus account identifier
Running balance versus end-of-day balance

Do not collapse every secondary date into “date” or every monetary field into “balance”.

A canonical schema should include typed extension fields for institution-specific data.

19. Do not force bank data exports through a statement-only model

Some supplied files are disclosure exports, investigation reports or processed datasets rather than conventional statements. They can lack:

Statement periods
Opening or closing balance labels
Printed totals
Page boundaries
Product names
Conventional transaction descriptions

They may instead have:

One account per sheet
Customer metadata sheets
Hidden sheets
Workbook instructions
Calculated controls
Location coordinates
Internal source classifications
One signed amount column
Formula-derived balances

The ingestion layer should classify these as bank_data_export, investigation_report, processed_dataset or customer_statement. Forcing all four into one statement model will create false assumptions.

20. Privacy cannot be an output-only filter

Given the density of customer information in these documents, privacy controls should operate at every stage:

Secure source storage
Restricted logs
No raw document text in application telemetry
Redaction before samples or debugging output
Structured masking based on field type
Removal of document metadata where appropriate
Safe exception messages
Restricted review screens
Audit trail for exports
Automated scanning of generated examples
Separate handling of location data and map links
No full card, account or customer identifiers in model prompts unless the processing environment is approved for them

The development test suite should use synthetic statements shaped like the source layouts, not copied customer transactions.

21. Produce confidence at field level, not just document level

A statement can be correctly classified while individual rows remain uncertain.

Confidence should be tracked for:

Layout classification
Statement boundary
Account-section assignment
Header mapping
Row boundary
Date
Direction
Amount
Balance
Description
Continuation attachment
Year inference
Total reconciliation

This enables sensible review rules, such as:

Automatically accept high-confidence, reconciled statements
Review only rows with ambiguous amount placement
Review statements whose totals do not reconcile
Review OCR pages with low-confidence dates
Review pages classified as unknown
22. Never silently “repair” the source

The tool should distinguish:

Extracted value
Normalised value
Inferred value
Corrected value
Derived value

Examples:

A year inferred from the statement period is inferred.
A balance calculated from prior balance and amount is derived.
An OCR-decimal correction is corrected.
A missing closing balance should remain missing, even if it can be calculated.
A transaction direction determined by the debit column is extracted from structure.

Users should be able to see how every output field was obtained.

23. Make the statement-design specification machine-readable

The current statement-design format is already capturing most of the right information, but I would convert each design into a formal schema.

Recommended sections:

Identity and recognition
Variant aliases
Source type
Statement-boundary rules
Page classifiers
Period parser
Balance parser
Table header variants
Column geometry
Row-start rules
Continuation rules
Amount-direction rules
Skip rules
End rules
Section rules
No-transaction rules
Validation rules
OCR substitutions
Privacy fields
Known limitations
Test fixtures

Avoid putting operationally important rules only in “Anything Else”. Every rule used by the parser should have a structured home.

24. Use declarative rules with limited custom code

A good architecture would be:

Generic document ingestion
Generic OCR and spreadsheet reading
Generic page segmentation
Declarative layout identification
Declarative table and row rules
Shared date and money parsers
Shared statement assembler
Shared validator
Small bank-specific hooks only where necessary

Most designs differ in configuration, not in fundamental processing logic.

Custom code should be reserved for genuinely unusual cases such as:

Bilingual duplicate headings
Category icons without text
Formula-supported spreadsheet rows
Multi-account composite statements
Unusual loan principal and interest component tables
25. Build a golden test corpus around edge cases, not average statements

For every layout family, include tests for:

One-page statement
Multi-page statement
No transactions
One transaction only
Only fees or interest
Credit balance
Overdrawn balance
Zero balance
Year rollover
Transaction date before period start
Different processed date
Foreign-currency purchase
Foreign-currency reversal
Refund or adjustment
Card replacement
Multiple cardholders
Missing heading on continuation page
Terms page inside the file sequence
OCR-only page
Row with no amount
Zero-value row
Repeated amount and description
Missing closing balance
Failed reconciliation
Out-of-order rows
Account closure
Literal "." or "-" in a numeric field
Spreadsheet formula unavailable

Tests should assert both what is extracted and what must not be extracted.

26. Validation should be visible in the output

Each converted statement should have a conversion report containing:

Detected institution and design
Recognition confidence
Statement period
Page or sheet membership
Transaction count
Printed totals
Calculated totals
Opening and closing balance
Balance reconciliation
Unparsed lines
Low-confidence fields
OCR corrections
Duplicate candidates
Excluded sections
Warnings
Source provenance

That report will be at least as important as the transaction output, particularly for FIU use where explainability and traceability matter.

27. The tool needs a controlled failure mode

A strong reader should prefer:

“Recognised statement, three rows require review”
“Statement assembled, transaction table not confidently located”
“Totals disagree by a specified amount”
“One page appears missing”
“Unsupported variant”
“Balance unavailable”
“Possible OCR corruption”

It should not:

Return an empty transaction file as if successful
Guess a product
invent a closing date
Convert missing values to zero
infer direction from descriptions when column evidence exists
discard rows that fail balance checks
merge apparent duplicates automatically
silently skip unidentified pages
28. The designs themselves need normalisation before implementation

The compiled file contains repeated layout families, variant-specific copies and some specification inconsistencies. Examples include numbering resets, alternative phrasing for the same location, designs with older and newer variants combined, and places where the formal choice vocabulary is not followed exactly.

Before coding, I would create:

One canonical layout-family record
Separate variant records
A product-alias table
A title-era table
Normalised column-role definitions
Normalised money-style definitions
A shared vocabulary for page and row types
A list of unresolved observations that still need source verification

Otherwise, the reader risks implementing the incidental structure of the notes rather than the underlying statement structure.

Bottom line

The conversion tool should be designed around this principle:

Classify and assemble first, extract second, reconcile third, normalise fourth, and never discard the source evidence.

The hardest parts are not reading dates and amounts. They are:

Determining statement boundaries
Distinguishing transactions from nearby financial summaries
Reconstructing rows across lines and pages
Resolving direction correctly
Handling layout changes over time
Preserving zero-value and reversal rows
Managing OCR and extraction disorder
Reconciling without overcorrecting
Maintaining privacy and provenance

If those are built into the architecture from the beginning, adding a new bank design becomes a controlled configuration and testing exercise. If they are not, every new statement will become another brittle exception.






